IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Domicile Act 1982 (Cth) — THE NOUNS, THE RULES AND THE TOP-LEVEL GOAL.
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/C2004A02560.txt (compilation of 25
-- January 2019). The person whose domicile is in question asks: "Can I have a
-- domicile of my own; as a child, whose domicile do I take; have I acquired a
-- domicile of choice?"

§ `Nouns`

-- s 9(1)(a): with whom the child has their principal home.
DECLARE `Principal home` IS ONE OF
    `with one of my parents`
    `with both of my parents`
    `with neither parent`

-- s 9(2): the adoptive parents I have on adoption.
DECLARE `Adoption` IS ONE OF
    `not adopted`
    `adopted by 2 parents`
    `adopted by 1 parent only`

-- The answer to "whose domicile do I take?" (s 9).
DECLARE `Child's domicile` IS ONE OF
    `the domicile the parent I live with has from time to time`
    `the domicile that parent had at death`
    `the domicile I would have if born in wedlock to my adoptive parents`
    `the domicile my sole adoptive parent has from time to time`
    `as the rescission order provides, otherwise as if I had not been adopted`

DECLARE `Domicile case` HAS
    `the time asked about`                                      IS A DATE
    `my date of birth`                                          IS A DATE
    -- s 4(1) child (b), s 8(1)(b)
    `I am or have been married`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 8(2)
    `I cannot acquire a domicile because of mental incapacity`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 9(1)
    `my principal home is`                                      IS A `Principal home`
    `my parents live separately and apart`                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have another living parent`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parent whose domicile I follow has died`               IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 9(2), (5)
    `my adoption`                                               IS AN `Adoption`
    `my adoption has been rescinded`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 9(4)
    `I had a domicile under s 9 when I ceased to be a child`    IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 10 (residence is the common law's other limb, asked as a fact)
    `I reside in the country`                                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `I intend to make my home indefinitely in the country`      IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Domicile position` HAS
    `I may have a domicile of my own`                           IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `as a child my domicile is`                                 IS A MAYBE `Child's domicile`
    `I keep my domicile from childhood`                         IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `I have acquired a domicile of choice in the country`       IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `my domicile follows my husband's`                          IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
    `my domicile of origin revives when I abandon my domicile of choice` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN

§ `Rules`

-- Commencement: 1 July 1982 (endnote 3: s 2 and gaz 1982, No G26).
`the commencement` MEANS Date 1 7 1982

-- s 5(1): "The domicile of a person at a time before the commencement of this
-- Act shall be determined as if this Act ... had not been enacted." (2): after
-- it, as if this Act had always been in force. The commencement day itself is
-- taken as after.
@ref s 5(1)-(2)
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act governs` c IF
    c's `the time asked about` AT LEAST `the commencement`

-- A person attains an age on the anniversary of birth; 29 February falls on
-- 28 February in a common year.
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am 18 or over` c IF
    `add months` (c's `my date of birth`) 216 AT MOST c's `the time asked about`

-- s 4(1): "child means a person who: (a) has not attained the age of 18
-- years; and (b) is not, and has not at any time been, married."
@ref s 4(1) child
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am a child` c IF
        NOT (`I am 18 or over` c)
    AND NOT (c's `I am or have been married`)

-- s 8(1): capable of an independent domicile if 18 or over, or is or has been
-- married, "and not otherwise". (2): not for a person incapable under the
-- common law because of mental incapacity; the Act then says nothing.
@ref s 8
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`may I have a domicile of my own` c MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the Act governs` c)) OR c's `I cannot acquire a domicile because of mental incapacity`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (NOT (`I am a child` c))

-- s 9(1): principal home with one parent, and the parents living separately
-- and apart or no other living parent: that parent's domicile from time to
-- time, or at death. (3): it ends when the child goes to live with the other
-- parent or the parents live together, which the present facts show.
@ref s 9(1), (3)
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 9(1) applies to` c IF
        c's `my principal home is` EQUALS `with one of my parents`
    AND (c's `my parents live separately and apart` OR NOT (c's `I have another living parent`))

-- s 9: "as a child, whose domicile do I take?" NOTHING where I am not a child,
-- before commencement, or where s 9 does not speak (the common law governs).
-- s 9(5) (rescission) displaces (2); (2)(b) (a sole adoptive parent) is
-- taken before (1), which then applies to any parents (changed after the
-- independent pass).
@ref s 9(1)-(3), (5)
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Child's domicile`
`whose domicile I take as a child` c MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the Act governs` c)) OR NOT (`I am a child` c)
            THEN NOTHING
        IF (NOT (c's `my adoption` EQUALS `not adopted`)) AND c's `my adoption has been rescinded`
            THEN JUST `as the rescission order provides, otherwise as if I had not been adopted`
        IF c's `my adoption` EQUALS `adopted by 1 parent only` AND c's `the parent whose domicile I follow has died`
            THEN JUST `the domicile that parent had at death`
        IF c's `my adoption` EQUALS `adopted by 1 parent only`
            THEN JUST `the domicile my sole adoptive parent has from time to time`
        IF `section 9(1) applies to` c AND c's `the parent whose domicile I follow has died`
            THEN JUST `the domicile that parent had at death`
        IF `section 9(1) applies to` c
            THEN JUST `the domicile the parent I live with has from time to time`
        IF c's `my adoption` EQUALS `adopted by 2 parents`
            THEN JUST `the domicile I would have if born in wedlock to my adoptive parents`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 9(4): a domicile held under s 9(1) or (2) on ceasing to be a child is kept
-- until a domicile of choice is acquired.
@ref s 9(4)
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`do I keep my domicile from childhood` c MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the Act governs` c)) OR `I am a child` c
            THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (c's `I had a domicile under s 9 when I ceased to be a child`)
            THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE `not if I have since chosen` c

GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`not if I have since chosen` c MEANS
    CONSIDER `have I acquired a domicile of choice` c
    WHEN JUST x THEN JUST (NOT x)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 10: the intention needed is "the intention to make his or her home
-- indefinitely in that country"; s 8: only a person capable of an independent
-- domicile can acquire one. Residence is the common law's other requirement.
@ref ss 8, 10
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`have I acquired a domicile of choice` c MEANS
    CONSIDER `may I have a domicile of my own` c
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST capable THEN JUST (    capable
                                 AND c's `I reside in the country`
                                 AND c's `I intend to make my home indefinitely in the country`)

-- s 6: the rule that a married woman has at all times her husband's domicile
-- is abolished.
@ref s 6
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`does my domicile follow my husband's` c MEANS
    IF `the Act governs` c THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING

-- s 7: the domicile of origin no longer revives on abandoning a domicile of
-- choice; the domicile continues until a different one is acquired.
@ref s 7
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`does my domicile of origin revive` c MEANS
    IF `the Act governs` c THEN JUST FALSE ELSE NOTHING

@ref ss 4-10
@export default The domicile position of a person under the Domicile Act 1982 (Cth), at a time on or after 1 July 1982: whether they can have a domicile of their own (s 8: 18 or over, or married at any time, unless mentally incapable); as a child, whose domicile they take (s 9: the parent they live with when the parents live apart, adoptive parents, rescission); whether they keep that domicile after ceasing to be a child (s 9(4)); whether they have acquired a domicile of choice in a country (s 10: residence and the intention to make their home there indefinitely); and that a wife's domicile no longer follows her husband's (s 6) and the domicile of origin no longer revives (s 7). NOTHING where the Act does not answer (before 1 July 1982, mental incapacity, or the common law). DRAFT, not reviewed by an Australian lawyer.
GIVEN c IS A `Domicile case`  @desc The time asked about, my age and marriage, where my principal home is, my parents and any adoption, and my residence and intention
GIVETH A `Domicile position`
`the domicile position for` c MEANS
    `Domicile position` WITH
        `I may have a domicile of my own`                       IS `may I have a domicile of my own` c
        `as a child my domicile is`                             IS `whose domicile I take as a child` c
        `I keep my domicile from childhood`                     IS `do I keep my domicile from childhood` c
        `I have acquired a domicile of choice in the country`   IS `have I acquired a domicile of choice` c
        `my domicile follows my husband's`                      IS `does my domicile follow my husband's` c
        `my domicile of origin revives when I abandon my domicile of choice` IS `does my domicile of origin revive` c
